hw/perf/ - timing / frequency performance tracking
===================================================

The record of how fast this design closes and WHY, so every iteration starts
from "where the wall is" instead of guessing.

    PERFLOG      hand-maintained trajectory: one entry per optimisation, with
                 the achieved Fmax, the limiting path, what changed, and the
                 next thing to attack. Read top-to-bottom to see the climb.

How to add a data point
-----------------------
    make -f hw.mk fmax                 # nexys_a7, no floorplan
    make -f hw.mk fmax FLOORPLAN=1     # with the PE-array pblock

fmax.tcl binary-searches the smallest clock period that still meets setup AND
hold, reports the achieved Fmax and the limiting path (the "wall"), and appends
a machine-readable row to build/vivado/fmax_history.csv (ephemeral -- build/ is
git-ignored). When a change moves the number, copy the headline into PERLOG with
a one-line note on what did it and what the new wall is.

Reading the wall
----------------
"single-cycle (control/reset/IO)" -> the datapath multicycle is NOT binding;
attack fanout / logic depth on that control path (e.g. reset distribution,
readback mux). "datapath (multicycle)" -> the two-cycle ALU path is binding
again; either raise the multicycle factor (slower step cadence, still one step =
one result) or shorten the operand-mux/DSP/op-mux chain. Fmax here is UART-bound
in practice, so a higher clock is about headroom and knowing the design's limit,
not throughput.
